WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:05.600 --> 00:00:09.480
China has never really been explicit in
precisely defining its claims in the

00:00:09.480 --> 00:00:13.440
South China Sea, or what it hopes to
achieve there. Still, it's possible to

00:00:13.440 --> 00:00:17.400
discern some of China's objectives, and
fundamentally I think China wants to

00:00:17.400 --> 00:00:21.500
control the waters of the South China Sea. It wants to dominate the South China Sea.

00:00:21.539 --> 00:00:25.740
Why does it want that? First, doing so
would make it easier for China to defend

00:00:25.740 --> 00:00:29.700
its southern coastline, which is home to
important economic centers on the mainland.

00:00:29.760 --> 00:00:33.899
Secondly, dominating the South China Sea would allow China to more easily defend

00:00:33.899 --> 00:00:37.920
against the blockades should a crisis ever
break out, while making it easier for it

00:00:37.920 --> 00:00:42.930
to blockade others, especially Taiwan, Japan, and even South Korea. Third, dominating

00:00:42.930 --> 00:00:46.379
the South China Sea would allow China to
more easily coerce and even project

00:00:46.379 --> 00:00:50.789
force against its Southeast Asian
neighbors. And lastly, dominating the

00:00:50.789 --> 00:00:54.780
South China Sea would give China a safe
bastion in which its ballistic missile

00:00:54.780 --> 00:00:58.829
submarines could operate. If its nuclear
submarines can patrol in the South China

00:00:58.829 --> 00:01:03.480
sea without having to worry about US
military assets tracking their every move,

00:01:03.480 --> 00:01:09.030
it enhances nuclear deterrence vis a vis the United States. So what are your

00:01:09.030 --> 00:01:11.640
thoughts about China's ambitions in the
South China Sea?

00:01:11.640 --> 00:01:15.150
Let us know in the comments, and also let us know what other topics you want

00:01:15.150 --> 00:01:16.860
AEI scholars to cover in 60 seconds.

